%I A402232 #19 Nov 19 2026 09:28:56
%S A402232 0,1,2,3,4,1,6,7,1,1,10,7,12,1,14,15,16,1,18,11,20,1,22,7,1,1,26,15,
%T A402232 28,1,30,31,32,1,34,19,36,1,38,31,40,1,42,23,19,1,46,31,1,1,50,27,52,
%U A402232 1,54,15,56,1,58,31,60,1,55,63,64,1,66,35,68,1,70,55,72
%N A402232 Let chi be the Dirichlet character modulo n sending k coprime to n to the signature of the permutation (k mod n, 2*k mod n, ..., (n-1)*k mod n). Then a(n) is the label of chi in the Conrey labeling system.
%C A402232 Here chi is the character Jacobi(.,n) for odd n, principal for n == 2 (mod 4), and induced by kronecker(-4,.) for n divisible by 4. The result for odd n is called Zolotarev's lemma; see A401152 for more information.
%H A402232 Jianing Song, <a href="/A402232/b402232.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A402232 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zolotarev%27s_lemma">Zolotarev's lemma</a>.
%F A402232 For odd n = p1^e1*...*pr^er, a(n) is the number x in [0,n-1] such that x == (-1)^ei (mod pi^ei) for all i. For even n = 2^e*s (s odd), a(n) is the number x in [0,n-1] such that x == -1 (mod 2^e) and x == 1 (mod s). In particular, a(n) = 1 if and only if n == 2 (mod 4) or n > 1 is a square, and a(n) = n-1 if and only if n is in A376218 or n is a power of 2.
%e A402232 For n = 12, chi is the product of kronecker(-4,.) (having label -1 modulo 4) and the principal character modulo 3. The label of chi must be congruent to -1 modulo 4 and 1 modulo 3, and so a(12) = 7.
%e A402232 For n = 40, chi is the product of kronecker(-4,.) (having label -1 modulo 8) and the principal character modulo 5. The label of chi must be congruent to -1 modulo 8 and 1 modulo 5, and so a(40) = 31.
%e A402232 For n = 63, chi = Jacobi(.,63) is the product of Jacobi(.,7) (having label -1 modulo 7) and Jacobi(.,9) (having label 1 modulo 9, since Jacobi(.,9) is principal). The label of chi must be congruent to -1 modulo 7 and 1 modulo 9, and so a(63) = 55.
%o A402232 (PARI) a(n) = if(n%2==1, my(f=factor(n), even_exp=1, odd_exp=1); for(i=1, #f~, if(f[i,2]%2==0, even_exp*=f[i,1]^f[i,2], odd_exp*=f[i,1]^f[i,2])); lift(chinese(Mod(-1, odd_exp), Mod(1, even_exp))), my(e=valuation(n, 2)); lift(chinese(Mod(-1, 2^e), Mod(1, n>>e))))
%Y A402232 Cf. A401152, A401153 (character values), A402272 (bisection).
%K A402232 nonn,easy,new
%O A402232 1,3
%A A402232 _Jianing Song_, Nov 16 2026
